// Dyret ("the beast"): Gunnar Bingen's Norwegian caddis, part Superpuppan and part Devil Bug.

id dyret
name "Dyret"
aliases "The Beast" "The Animal"
type emerger
imitates caddis
naturals caddis-pupa caddis-adult
difficulty beginner
species trout grayling
season june july august september
origin originator="Gunnar Bingen" year=1991 region="Rena, Norway"
description "A clump of deer hair bound along the shank, its tips a tuft behind and its butts a square stub in front, with a dubbed body and a long palmered hackle trimmed underneath."
history """
    Gunnar Bingen, who lives by the Rena, designed it around 1991 (his design sheet is dated
    that year) as a mix of the Superpuppan and the Devil Bug: in his words the Superpuppan
    "lacked" something in some situations, and the Devil Bug was efficient but fragile.
    Thomas Züllich notes that, though meant as a caddis emerger, it has earned its place in
    many other situations too. Torben Meldgaard captioned his
    photo of it "extremely ugly but highly efficient".
    """
fishing """
    Bingen likes best to fish dry late in the evening, when big trout move in along the bank
    to feed on caddis. Many tyers, Bingen among them, clip the hackle underneath;
    Thomas Züllich leaves it full. Popular with trout and grayling anglers across Scandinavia,
    and Markus Hoffman calls his version a killer for northern grayling.
    """
sources {
    "Sexyloops: Fly Tying School part 27, Dyret (Thomas Züllich)" url="https://www.sexyloops.com/index.php/ps/sexyloops-fly-tying-school-part-27-dyret"
    "GlobalFlyFisher: Dyret (video, Hans Weilenmann)" url="https://globalflyfisher.com/video/dyret"
    "GlobalFlyFisher: Dyret, the Animal (video, Markus Hoffman)" url="https://globalflyfisher.com/video/dyret-the-animal"
    "GlobalFlyFisher: Dyret, the Beast (video)" url="https://globalflyfisher.com/video/dyret-the-beast"
    "GlobalFlyFisher: Dyret (photo, Torben Meldgaard)" url="https://globalflyfisher.com/picture/dyret"
}
// Photos for checking proportions while authoring (pnpm refcheck); never published.
references {
    "https://www.sexyloops.com/upload/images/tz/dyret/192ecde3c27bd81f4fefda80944fed1f.jpg" page="https://www.sexyloops.com/index.php/ps/sexyloops-fly-tying-school-part-27-dyret" license="All rights reserved; reference only" note="Side view, before the hackle is clipped"
    "https://img.youtube.com/vi/ucDmVgpugMs/maxresdefault.jpg" page="https://globalflyfisher.com/video/dyret" author="Hans Weilenmann" license="All rights reserved; reference only" note="Side view, clipped underneath"
    "https://globalflyfisher.com/sites/default/files/article_pictures/4_5.jpg" page="https://globalflyfisher.com/picture/dyret" author="Torben Meldgaard" license="All rights reserved; reference only" note="Side view in the vise"
}

hook dry-standard size=12
thread brown size="8/0"

materials {
    hair deer-hair
    hackle cock-hackle color=brown fibre=1.8
    body dry-fly-dubbing color=olive
}

steps {
    attach-thread at=0.9
    // From the photos: tips about 0.6 of the shank behind the bend.
    tie-in hair length=3.0 angle=2 spread=35 note="A small stacked clump on top, tips back, bound down along the whole shank as an underbody."
    wrap-thread to=0.0
    tie-in hackle note="By the stripped stem, at the bend."
    dub body to=0.9 note="Over the bound-down hair, to just behind the eye."
    wind hackle to=0.9 turns=6 note="Open turns, a long hackle."
    tie-off hackle
    whip-finish note="Behind the deer-hair butts."
    trim hair leave=0.2 note="Straight across, leaving a short square stub in front of the eye."
    trim hackle keep=top
}
